      "Colonel Wright's 1858 campaign was brutal. After defeating the tribes at the Battle of Four Lakes and the Battle of Spokane Plains, his men rounded up the Palouse and Spokane horse herds and shot them over two days, a calculated blow to the tribes' way of life. He then hanged several leaders at a creek south of the city, which became known as Hangman Creek. The site of the horse slaughter is marked by a monument east of Spokane.",
